This guide is for the people who write and change the content of a website in Mica. You need no technical knowledge. "Help for editors" in the menu of Mica opens this page.

## Save and Publish

Mica has two steps.

1. **Save** keeps your change in Mica. The website does not change yet. The page then shows "Saved · not published".
2. **Publish** sends your saved changes to the website. The website is built again, which usually takes a few minutes.

![The form of a project, with "Unsaved changes" and the Save changes button at the top](media/entry.png)

Save often. A saved change is safe: you can close the browser and come back tomorrow. Pressing Enter in a text field also saves.

A field with a red star (*) is required. You can save without it, but you cannot publish until it has a value. A grey field is read-only: you can read it, but not change it.

## Other editors see your saved changes

All editors of a website work on the same saved changes. A colleague sees your saved change on the Publish page, and can publish it. Tell your team before you publish changes that are not yours.

## Publish your changes

Open **Publish** in the menu. The page shows each saved change, with the value **Before** and **After**.

![The Publish page: one saved change, its value before and after, and the field "Say what changed"](media/publish.png)

1. Choose the changes to publish. The changes that you do not choose stay saved.
2. Write a short note in **Say what changed**, for example "New opening hours".
3. Select **Publish**.

Mica then says "Your website is being rebuilt now." When the website is ready depends on your hosting, usually a few minutes. If Mica says "Published in Mica, not on the website yet", it tries again every minute. You do not have to do anything.

## Discard a change

To remove a saved change, select **Discard changes** on the entry, or **Discard** next to the change on the Publish page. The entry goes back to the version on the website.

## Two saves at the same time

When a colleague saved the same entry while you were editing it, Mica shows "Somebody else saved this while you were editing", with their version and yours. Choose:

- **Keep mine**: your version replaces theirs. Mica notes who chose it.
- **Use theirs**: your changes go, and you see their version.

When the website changed after you saved, for example because a developer changed the same text, the Publish page shows it. Choose **Keep my version** or **Use the website's version**.

## When your session ends

After some hours, you must sign in again. When that happens while you edit, Mica keeps your changes in the browser tab. Sign in again, and select **Restore them**.

## Images and files

![The Media page: the files in folders, with a preview and a box to select each one](media/media.png)

- Upload an image in its field, or on the **Media** page.
- Write the alt text in the field, under the image. The alt text tells people who cannot see the image what it shows. It belongs to the place where the image is used, so the same image can have a different alt text on another page.
- You can delete a file on the Media page when no entry uses it. Mica tells you which entries use a file.
- You cannot rename a file. Upload it again with the new name, and change the entries that use it.

## Tables and images in the text

When a text field shows the Table and Image buttons, you can add them to the text.

- **Table** adds a table with a header row. In a table, a second row of buttons adds and deletes rows and columns. Each cell holds one line of text.
- **Image** opens the media library. Choose or upload an image, then write its alt text. To change the alt text later, select the image and select **Image** again.
- Save a new entry once before you add an image to its text.

Some tables and images that a developer wrote stay read-only, for example an image inside a line of text. Mica then says why.

## Order and links between entries

- In a list that has a handle (⋮⋮) at the start of each row, drag the rows into the order of the website. This is the same everywhere: the entries of a collection, a list of items in a form, the chosen entries of a field such as the team of a project, and the files of a media field. With the keyboard, press Space on the handle, move with the arrow keys, and press Space again. The arrows at the end of a row move it one step. For files, they move it one step earlier or later.
- A field that names other entries, such as the team of a project, finds an entry as you type its name.

## Undo a published change

To undo a change that is on the website, change the entry back and publish again. To undo a whole publish, ask your developer: Git has each version of the website, and a developer can restore one. See [Scope](/docs/scope/).

## When something does not work

Open **System** in the menu, and select **Copy report**. Send the report to your developer. It contains no passwords.
